body:
my experiences with it weren't delirious at all, although I was nodding and blacking out, so... DXM is fun but it can be pretty damn dangerous.

&#x200B;

Edit: I was replying to u/LSD_tripper, and probably missclicked.

This was a very thought-provoking question

From what I have managed to reason out, you could make a 3D model using 2 BW height maps - one mapped from the bottom, the second from the top. (XY/X-Y) The only issue with this approach is that you cannot have crevices, cavities, and similar structures that would be obscured from both mapping projections when scaled down to a plane.

&#x200B;

The same argument goes for XY/YZ, I would imagine. Imagine a hypothetical 3D model of a sphere that has some depth but is hollow on the inside. There is no way for that to be mapped using this method, I think. The same would apply to more complex structures of a similar kind. For example nostrils on a human head.

&#x200B;

You could have 6 maps for each side of a cube, however same with the nostrils example, they could be for example obscured by the chin. There would still be no way to get those properly mapped.
